# Community Event OS — Backend Cursor Rules

## Project
Multi-tenant event platform API. Started as London Community Fest backend, designed to serve any community organisation. Built by a solo founder — code quality over speed.

## Stack
- Python 3.13
- Django 6.0 (latest)
- Django REST Framework 3.17
- PostgreSQL (production via Railway)
- SQLite (local development)

## Architecture Rules
- Every data model connects to Event, every Event connects to Organisation
- All views that touch event-scoped data MUST inherit from EventScopedMixin (common/mixins.py)
- URL pattern: /api/v1/events/{event_slug}/... — event slug scopes everything
- Business logic lives in services/ not in views — views are thin (under 30 lines)
- Settings split: config/settings/base.py, development.py, production.py
- Environment variables loaded via python-dotenv — never hardcode secrets

## Folder Structure (enforced)
- config/ — project settings, urls, wsgi only
- config/settings/ — base.py, development.py, production.py
- apps/core/ — Organisation, Event, OrganisationMembership models
- apps/waitlist/ — email capture
- apps/tickets/ — orders, tickets, Stripe, QR codes
- apps/vendors/ — vendor applications with status workflow
- apps/sponsors/ — sponsor leads with status tracking
- apps/checkin/ — event-day QR scanning and stats
- common/ — shared mixins, permissions, pagination, exceptions
- requirements/ — base.txt, dev.txt, prod.txt

## Django App Rules
- Each app has: models.py, serializers.py, views.py, urls.py, admin.py
- Complex logic gets a services.py or services/ directory (e.g. apps/tickets/services/stripe.py)
- Models use explicit related_name on all ForeignKey fields
- All models that belong to an event have: event = models.ForeignKey("core.Event", on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name="...")
- Use choices for status fields — define them as class-level constants on the model
- Add __str__ methods to every model
- Register every model in admin.py

## Serializer Rules
- Use ModelSerializer for standard CRUD
- Use plain Serializer for custom input (e.g. checkout requests)
- Validate at serializer level, not view level
- Never expose internal IDs in API responses — use slugs or reference codes
- Include read_only_fields explicitly

## View Rules
- Use DRF generic views (CreateAPIView, ListAPIView, RetrieveAPIView) not ViewSets
- Inherit EventScopedMixin for all event-scoped views
- Keep views under 30 lines — move logic to services
- Set permission_classes explicitly on every view
- Use get_object_or_404, never bare .get() that might 500

## API Response Rules
- Success: return serialized data directly
- Created: return 201 with created object
- Errors: return {"detail": "message"} or {"field": ["error"]}
- Never return raw Python tracebacks
- Use custom exception handler from common/exceptions.py

## Security Rules
- No hardcoded secrets — use environment variables
- Stripe webhook signature verification required
- Rate limiting on public endpoints (waitlist, vendor forms)
- CORS restricted to frontend domain in production
- No raw SQL — use Django ORM

## Code Quality
- No any types in type hints
- No print() — use logging module
- No bare except: — always catch specific exceptions
- Add docstrings to models, services, and non-obvious views
- Keep imports sorted: stdlib, third-party, django, local apps

## What NOT to Do
- Do not use ViewSets or routers — use explicit URL patterns
- Do not put business logic in views — use services
- Do not hardcode event IDs — use EventScopedMixin
- Do not create custom User model yet — use Django's built-in auth
- Do not add Celery, Redis, or WebSockets — not needed yet
- Do not write frontend code in this repo
